#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069817} :activity/id #uuid "e575068f-304a-506b-9458-1523c052c613" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068637 :question/id #uuid "3ecf6cf7-468d-53d9-b52f-6aa69ce45306" :question/math-academy-id "e-7054" :question/problem "Solving the equation $25x^{2}-20x+4= 0$ using the quadratic formula leads to the expression below:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = \\dfrac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{(-20)^2-4\\cdot  25 \\cdot  \\boxed{ {\\phantom{00}}}}} {2 \\cdot 25}\n$$\nWhat number is missing from the formula, and what is the solution of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na=25, \\quad b=-20, \\quad c=4\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-20) ± \\sqrt{(-20)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot (4)})/(2 \\cdot 25) \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{20^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot \\color{blue}\\,4\\,}}{2 \\cdot 25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\boxed{ { {\\color{blue}\\hspace{0.167em} 4 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}.$\n\nNow, to find the solution, we simplify the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{20^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot \\color{blue}\\,4\\,}}{2 \\cdot 25} &= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{400 - 400}}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{0}}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± 0}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93dea6c-b664-523e-a52f-985792115895" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048868}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680803}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678556} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678553}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quadratic Formula Using a Framework"} :step/id #uuid "0e329ac2-97a6-53b1-95fe-6baa18d4a70a" :step/math-academy-id 26005 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069818}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069948 :tutorial/content "The **quadratic formula** is a formula that we can use to compute the solution of a quadratic equation.\n\nGiven a quadratic equation in standard form\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nthe formula for the solutions to this equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic formula is derived using a method called *completing the square*. You'll learn more about this method and how it's used to derive the quadratic formula in future lessons.\n\nTo see how applying the quadratic formula works in practice, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\nx^2-2x-15=0\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of this quadratic equation are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a=1}, \\qquad {\\color{red}b=-2}, \\qquad {\\color{purple}c=-15}\n$$\n\nTo compute the solution, we substitute the above values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(-15)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{64}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± 8}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 4 = 5 \\\\\nx &= 1 - 4 =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our quadratic equation are $x=-3$ and $x=5$." :tutorial/id #uuid "16946bb7-8ddb-5ae7-a433-de58df59febf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1629 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b1ed2594-7cf6-57f4-bc81-3b89ff11faa4" :step/math-academy-id 4486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068862 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068500 :question/id #uuid "fe2f52aa-f883-5455-8d35-94ddc6a5cb1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1434" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $2x^2 - 3x - 5 = 0$." :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na = 2, \\quad b = -3, \\quad c = -5\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-(-3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4(2)(-5)}}{2(2)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{49}}{4} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{3 \\pm 7}{4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{3 + 7}{4} = \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3 - 7}{4} =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-1$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "773cb449-9efe-5c0c-95ca-e433d8557efc" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680803}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678512} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678510}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Quadratic Formula to Solve a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "2361dbb5-1b9b-5ce9-95ec-f0f055d1a594" :step/math-academy-id 4487 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068501 :question/id #uuid "e1662bab-c11d-5770-8b7d-6646902f7e20" :question/math-academy-id "e-1435" :question/problem "Solve the equation $x^2 + 2x = 6$." :question/worked-solution "The first step is to put the equation into standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x &= 6 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x - 6 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na = 1, \\quad b = 2, \\quad c = -6\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-(2) \\pm \\sqrt{(2)^2 - 4(1)(-6)}}{2(1)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{28}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 2\\sqrt{7}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= -1 \\pm \\sqrt{7} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = -1 + \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx = -1 - \\sqrt{7}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c011eb9-7c5a-5180-812a-e6bbfcc7d93f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049077}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680803}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069271}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying and Solving a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "2f4ba00b-e0cb-527e-b317-77580216be52" :step/math-academy-id 4490 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068502 :question/id #uuid "9812207c-cb3b-5522-a08a-c0bf4672a22e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1436" :question/problem "What are the solutions to $3x^2 = x - 10$?" :question/worked-solution "The first step is to put the equation into standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - x + 10 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients are\n\n$$\na = 3, \\quad b = -1, \\quad c = 10\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-1) ± \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} - 4(3)(10)})/(2(3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 ± \\sqrt{1 - 120}}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 ± \\sqrt{-119}}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that the equation has no real solutions because $\\sqrt{-119}$ is not a real number."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf857de-6a58-5450-9fab-2795f1317e0b" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680803}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069349}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quadratic Formula To an Equation With No Real Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "9bb1a622-6413-5d78-9841-3800eebb1a9d" :step/math-academy-id 4494}] :activity/title "The Quadratic Formula" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068955}]}
